  "output": "Spec ID: token-efficient-tool-responses-ticket01-spec\nRevision: r39 (document: r39-replacement)\nFindings by ID: C7\n\n**C7 — MAJOR; high confidence — R6/R7/R10, AC38, T2→T3. New r39 repair consequence.**\n\n**Contradiction:** AC38 now requires an “admitted upstream call” retired “through the production trigger,” then resumed to observe `result_namespace_retired`. Its owning T2 remains “store-only and handler-free”; T3 explicitly owns delivery and inbound retirement wiring.\n\n**Cheap false pass:** substitute fixture-driven store retirement for the production trigger and claim AC38 passed. That proves store mechanics, not the required lifecycle. Conversely, the literal production test cannot pass at T2 using its declared prerequisites.\n\n**Evidence:** spec §4 AC38 versus §5 T2/T3; existing `internal/service/inbound.go:1152–1165` cleans projection ownership and SDK sessions, without retention integration.\n\n**Closing criterion:** reconcile AC38’s phase boundary so every T2 assertion is executable using T1/T2 artifacts alone, while the complete production-trigger→resumed-result sequence remains mandatory when that wiring exists. Synthetic owner reinjection must not substitute for production evidence.\n\nThis is not C6 reopening: its behavioral contradiction is repaired, but its newly assigned acceptance gate is infeasible.\n\n### Verified closures\n\n- **C6:** R6, AC38/43 and §3 handoff now forbid resumed-byte retention, handle issuance, aggregate increase or namespace resurrection after empty retirement and after tombstone clearing. Merely making an issued handle unusable no longer passes.\n- Singleton live-record retirement remains release-funded; marker-only retirement releases 256 bytes and replaces it with the tombstone. No fourth bookkeeping class is introduced.\n- **r38 ledger advisory:** the named stale AC38 retry pointer is removed; remaining historical wording has explicit supersession provenance.\n- R10’s isolated terminal fixtures remain mandatory; retirement does not eliminate the other typed paths.\n\n### Per-requirement verdicts\n\nR1 PASS; R2 PASS; R3 PASS; R4 PASS;  \nR5 PASS; R6 REVISE (C7); R7 REVISE (C7); R8 PASS;  \nR9 PASS; R10 REVISE (C7); R11 PASS; R12 PASS;  \nR13 PASS; R14 PASS; R15 PASS; R16 PASS.\n\nGolden bytes defeat payload-only measurement and short-prefix shortcuts; invocation counts defeat re-execution; reload, precedence, registry and reconstruction oracles cover their respective cheap passes. T1/T3/T4/T5 name falsifiable checks; T2’s production-lifecycle check requires amendment.\n\n8192 passes unchanged; 8193 pages. Bounds include escaped, serialized `CallToolResult` blocks and metadata, excluding JSON-RPC framing. Indices are 1-based; byte totals concern original serialized bytes, not runes. Execution error status remains preserved.\n\n### Coverage and limits\n\nFull candidate, shared risk matrix, decisions and r38/r39 observations read; ownership anchors independently checked. No silent OD1–OD3 resolution found. §7 retains existing infrastructure and AC13/14 emission/scrape checks.\n\nBinary-only proof cannot establish R1’s no-remarshal mechanism, R4’s entropy provenance, R6’s structural reclamation or R12’s exact internal registries; R15 also needs document inspection.\n\nNo writes or tests executed; supplied hashes could not be independently recomputed. Request configured `spec_critic_escalated` review of the substantive T2-feasibility disagreement. No execution approval granted.\n\n### Verdict\nREVISE\n",
